WEBVTT

1
00:13:18.520 --> 00:13:20.480
Hi, I'm

2
00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:26.560
Maria Garay, and I'm a PhD student
at the University of Salamanca in Spain.

3
00:13:26.600 --> 00:13:30.640
And what I'm going to talk about here
is part of my PhD,

4
00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:34.600
which in general— as a general project,
aims to look

5
00:13:34.600 --> 00:13:38.600
at the aesthetics
and politics of code poetry.

6
00:13:38.600 --> 00:13:42.400
I don't want to get too much
into classifications,

7
00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:47.800
but my focus on code poetry
is specifically in that which is written

8
00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:53.000
in programing languages, whose text
is made up of syntactically correct

9
00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:58.640
and potentially executable code, 
and whether it produces an output or not.

10
00:13:58.640 --> 00:14:02.440
But for the sake of clarity,
things like [?]

11
00:14:02.440 --> 00:14:06.680
and some of Jodi's works
would be, for example,

12
00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:10.000
outside of the scope of my research.

13
00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:15.560
And what I'm going to talk about
is also quite different

14
00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:19.040
to what I initially proposed,
because recently I've come into an avenue

15
00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:22.040
of looking at the relationship
between code poetry and labor that

16
00:14:22.040 --> 00:14:25.920
I thought would be more exciting
to explore here.

17
00:14:26.200 --> 00:14:30.360
One of the tasks
that I've been addressing

18
00:14:30.520 --> 00:14:34.160
recently in my PhD
has been that of seeing

19
00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:38.480
how code poetry integrates
into the wider genealogies of poetry.

20
00:14:38.520 --> 00:14:44.600
And it turns out that this integration
is also deeply linked with labor.

21
00:14:44.600 --> 00:14:48.800
There are two instances
that I will pinpoint today

22
00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:50.560
to approach this relationship.

23
00:14:50.560 --> 00:14:55.480
One kind of in the most recent history
related to code poetry,

24
00:14:55.480 --> 00:14:59.240
and then another one looking further back
into the origins of poetry

25
00:14:59.240 --> 00:15:02.360
and how both of these
are connected through the idea of labor.

26
00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:04.640
So the first

27
00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:08.960
instance that I wanted to look at
is the seed of code poetry as a genre,

28
00:15:09.640 --> 00:15:13.480
which I trace back to “Just another Perl
hacker” program.

29
00:15:13.520 --> 00:15:14.960
And here.

30
00:15:14.960 --> 00:15:18.640
So I work in Spain,
and I've never heard the acronym for “Just

31
00:15:18.640 --> 00:15:20.320
another Perl hacker”
pronounced in English.

32
00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:24.160
So if I completely made up
the pronunciation of this acronym,

33
00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:25.400
please feel free to let me know.

34
00:15:25.400 --> 00:15:28.400
I've been calling it “japes.”
I don't know if that's right.

35
00:15:29.360 --> 00:15:31.480
But, yeah,

36
00:15:31.480 --> 00:15:35.080
“japes.” Short programs
whose only function

37
00:15:35.080 --> 00:15:38.600
is to print the sentence “Just
another Perl hacker” into a console.

38
00:15:38.600 --> 00:15:42.800
And just in case
this is not familiar to everyone,

39
00:15:42.920 --> 00:15:46.920
they became really popular amongst
Perl programmers to use as signatures

40
00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:50.960
and emails or online forums
when Perl came into use.

41
00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:55.320
And “Just another Perl hacker” programs,
they have to be brief enough

42
00:15:55.320 --> 00:15:56.880
to fit a signature.

43
00:15:56.880 --> 00:16:00.360
And they tend to consist of creatively

44
00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:04.320
obfuscated code,
so they are formally quite constrained.

45
00:16:04.720 --> 00:16:10.440
This premise is really simple,
but precisely because of that,

46
00:16:10.440 --> 00:16:15.760
when you look at the village of “japes”
that ended up being produced, you find a

47
00:16:17.080 --> 00:16:17.880
really wide

48
00:16:17.880 --> 00:16:21.920
scope of experiments
which are really similar

49
00:16:21.920 --> 00:16:26.120
to those in concrete
poetry and European constraint literature.

50
00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:30.400
For example,

51
00:16:30.680 --> 00:16:34.800
you get “japes” that only use characters

52
00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:38.720
that don't exceed their writing line,

53
00:16:38.720 --> 00:16:44.800
so that don't go either
above or under kind of their, their line.

54
00:16:44.800 --> 00:16:48.800
This was itself a constraint
proposed by members of the Oulipo

55
00:16:48.800 --> 00:16:53.200
when they imagined a man that had access
to very little paper,

56
00:16:53.200 --> 00:16:57.280
and that in order to maximize the—

57
00:16:57.280 --> 00:16:59.680
optimize
the amount of space that they were using,

58
00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:03.280
they could only use characters
that didn't go above or below,

59
00:17:03.280 --> 00:17:07.400
and someone re-imagined this constraint
in Perl.

60
00:17:07.400 --> 00:17:11.720
For example, there are “japes”
that only use— that are written only

61
00:17:11.720 --> 00:17:17.960
using punctuation, or there are others
in which the code itself is ASCII art.

62
00:17:18.200 --> 00:17:22.320
So it's a mix of things
that were happening

63
00:17:22.320 --> 00:17:27.440
already in concrete poetry and Oulipian
literature that kind of end up

64
00:17:27.440 --> 00:17:30.760
bleeding into

65
00:17:30.760 --> 00:17:35.560
what people were doing with “japes.”
And the reason I'm going to “japes”

66
00:17:35.560 --> 00:17:40.880
is because these already
look or like, feel like

67
00:17:42.400 --> 00:17:44.560
proto code poems, right?

68
00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:47.040
Or not
even proto, just like proper code poems.

69
00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:53.440
But as a genre, the first code poem
that is recognized as such

70
00:17:53.920 --> 00:17:59.600
is this famous haiku by Larry Wall.

71
00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:04.480
Which was produced

72
00:18:04.480 --> 00:18:10.280
as a dare to him, or was proposed
as a dare to him by Sharon Hopkins.

73
00:18:10.280 --> 00:18:12.040
And then she was kind of the one that took

74
00:18:12.040 --> 00:18:16.040
it further and became the catalyst
for a lot more Perl poetry being made.

75
00:18:16.040 --> 00:18:20.640
She kind of popularized the genre,
and that's why this one is considered

76
00:18:20.880 --> 00:18:24.560
the first one, I would say,
although that's a bit nebulous.

77
00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:30.640
But the reason why “japes” seem special
is because they gave programmers

78
00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:33.640
a form in which the code that they wrote

79
00:18:33.640 --> 00:18:37.040
had the explicit role of being read.

80
00:18:37.040 --> 00:18:40.480
This wasn't kind of a secondary thing
to their function,

81
00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:43.240
but it was because the function was given
for all of them.

82
00:18:43.240 --> 00:18:48.200
They all just had to produce to print
“Just another Perl hacker” into a console.

83
00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:50.440
Then the focus was put elsewhere.

84
00:18:50.440 --> 00:18:54.680
The important part is what
the code itself looks like, and that it—

85
00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:58.880
and that they serve
as kind of a personal signature style.

86
00:18:59.120 --> 00:19:02.600
It's code made explicitly to be seen
and to be recognized

87
00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:05.600
as someone's artwork.

88
00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:10.480
With this story, I want to make 
that most recent connection with labor,

89
00:19:10.480 --> 00:19:13.480
and that is that code poetry stems

90
00:19:13.480 --> 00:19:18.800
not from poets or artists
that were looking to express themselves

91
00:19:18.800 --> 00:19:23.920
in a new medium and then arrived to code,
but rather from workers of code

92
00:19:23.960 --> 00:19:29.080
that being intimately acquainted
with their material, began using it for

93
00:19:29.080 --> 00:19:34.320
aesthetic and playful and ludic
means beyond code’s executable function.

94
00:19:34.360 --> 00:19:40.000
So we can see here how code poetry happens
as an excess of work.

95
00:19:40.040 --> 00:19:45.600
And this is the idea that kind of gives
name to what— my talk today.

96
00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:48.680
That's kind of half of the story.

97
00:19:48.680 --> 00:19:52.600
And then the other story
that I have been focusing on recently

98
00:19:52.600 --> 00:19:58.280
and that I established
a bit of a tentative parallelism

99
00:19:58.480 --> 00:20:02.320
with the one I've just told
is a much more older one.

100
00:20:03.160 --> 00:20:06.360
In when when understanding
how code poetry is part

101
00:20:06.360 --> 00:20:10.400
of a longer
literary history, I have ended up looking

102
00:20:11.560 --> 00:20:15.000
to much
more ancient times that I had predicted,

103
00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:19.680
and have ended up turning to the pre
literate and prehistorical origins

104
00:20:19.680 --> 00:20:23.840
of poetry itself,
which seems a bit excessive,

105
00:20:23.880 --> 00:20:30.000
a bit of a stretch, but surprisingly,
this is where it has taken me.

106
00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:33.080
And I will try to explain

107
00:20:33.080 --> 00:20:36.400
how both histories echo each other.

108
00:20:36.800 --> 00:20:40.560
The one of code poetry
and then poetry in general.

109
00:20:40.920 --> 00:20:46.280
I arrived at this
because there's a Marxist literary

110
00:20:46.280 --> 00:20:52.080
critic called George Thomson,
who in the mid 20th century suggested

111
00:20:52.080 --> 00:20:55.920
that there is a strict link between labor
and the emergence of poetry.

112
00:20:55.960 --> 00:21:00.080
His hypothesis was that 
when hominids were learning

113
00:21:00.120 --> 00:21:03.840
to use heavy tools
and trying to coordinate collective labor,

114
00:21:04.160 --> 00:21:09.600
they kind of reflexively emitted
these rhythmic and repetitive grunts.

115
00:21:09.640 --> 00:21:13.320
So everyone was lifting these heavy things
and at the same time,

116
00:21:13.360 --> 00:21:16.680
and needed this coordinating apparatus.

117
00:21:16.680 --> 00:21:21.440
And as the use of tools is perfected,

118
00:21:21.480 --> 00:21:26.400
these sounds become more articulate,
but they also become unnecessary

119
00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:31.720
because they no longer need
this sonorous apparatus to coordinate it.

120
00:21:32.200 --> 00:21:35.480
But he says that this

121
00:21:37.120 --> 00:21:41.320
sound structure survives in

122
00:21:41.320 --> 00:21:45.360
the rituals, then performed as a rehearsal
for a task carried out.

123
00:21:45.360 --> 00:21:50.920
So when when the sound doesn't
directly accompany the task, it

124
00:21:50.960 --> 00:21:55.400
branches out to become a mimetic dance

125
00:21:55.400 --> 00:21:58.400
that prepares people

126
00:22:00.960 --> 00:22:04.160
for the— for a successful reality.

127
00:22:04.200 --> 00:22:07.320
So the rituals have a function
akin to magic.

128
00:22:07.320 --> 00:22:11.080
It is performed in order to

129
00:22:12.080 --> 00:22:15.000
improve the task that is going to happen

130
00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:21.000
by some symbolic link between the dance
and the task itself.

131
00:22:21.080 --> 00:22:25.200
So Thomson's theory
is that formally patterned poetic

132
00:22:25.200 --> 00:22:30.400
speech is born out of work with tools,
and the idea that you can effect reality

133
00:22:30.400 --> 00:22:34.600
with incantation the same way you
can effect it with a manufactured tool.

134
00:22:34.840 --> 00:22:38.280
This is a beautiful proposal, of course,
but it remains hypothetical

135
00:22:38.280 --> 00:22:41.280
and also written in 1945.

136
00:22:41.320 --> 00:22:46.960
But more recent studies,
like Christopher Collins’s “Paleopoetics,”

137
00:22:47.080 --> 00:22:51.440
show that word and tool
do actually come together in ritual,

138
00:22:51.440 --> 00:22:57.360
and that in that form, speech
becomes a similar artifact to the tool.

139
00:22:57.400 --> 00:23:01.960
So he makes the comparison between
conversational speech and poetic speech

140
00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:07.080
in the sense that a conversational speech
works as a found tool.

141
00:23:07.080 --> 00:23:10.880
So the way you would find a stick
and maybe use it for a bit on your walk

142
00:23:10.920 --> 00:23:12.400
through— on your hike
through the mountains,

143
00:23:12.400 --> 00:23:15.840
but then discard it, versus
how poetic speech

144
00:23:15.840 --> 00:23:19.760
becomes an artifact
that you keep and reuse.

145
00:23:19.760 --> 00:23:24.440
And that sticks
the same way when you make a knife,

146
00:23:24.480 --> 00:23:25.520
you're not going to throw it out.

147
00:23:25.520 --> 00:23:28.520
You're going to keep it
because it's useful again and again.

148
00:23:28.520 --> 00:23:32.400
So in these manufactured artifacts

149
00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:38.600
that are poetic 
objects, rhythm and repetition

150
00:23:38.600 --> 00:23:42.240
are preserved as characteristics
of their kind of speech.

151
00:23:42.240 --> 00:23:47.880
And in being performed again and again
as rituals,

152
00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:52.920
these form into formal patterns and become
more structured as they get repeated.

153
00:23:52.920 --> 00:23:57.960
So from this kind of second, more ancient
story of the prehistory of poetry,

154
00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:03.560
I want to derive the idea
that in many ways, poetry is born

155
00:24:03.600 --> 00:24:09.120
out of an act that aspires to be
executable, so that poetry has always

156
00:24:09.640 --> 00:24:12.120
did aspire to be a bit of a program

157
00:24:12.120 --> 00:24:16.160
that can effect reality
the same way at all— does.

158
00:24:16.200 --> 00:24:21.040
The history of executable speech
and the idea that symbols

159
00:24:21.040 --> 00:24:25.240
can be transmuted into action
is well known and really long.

160
00:24:25.240 --> 00:24:31.120
For example, Florian Cramer
retells a quite comprehensive

161
00:24:31.160 --> 00:24:34.360
kind of catalog of this story in his book
Words Made Flesh.

162
00:24:34.400 --> 00:24:38.880
So it's not really that
what I'm saying here is new at all,

163
00:24:38.880 --> 00:24:43.080
but my aim is to orient
the understanding of the origins of code

164
00:24:43.080 --> 00:24:47.080
poetry as an excess born
out of the peripheries of labor

165
00:24:47.120 --> 00:24:51.800
that kind of turn to aesthetic purposes,
non instrumental, nonfunctional aesthetic

166
00:24:51.800 --> 00:24:56.720
purposes, and also to show how this echoes
the origins of poetry itself,

167
00:24:56.760 --> 00:25:02.120
that executable speech stands out
of the desire to work, to change reality.

168
00:25:02.120 --> 00:25:06.360
And from it we get the formal patterning
that historically governs

169
00:25:06.360 --> 00:25:10.880
poetic speech and that code,
which is the executable

170
00:25:10.880 --> 00:25:14.800
speech of today—
executable texts of today.

171
00:25:14.840 --> 00:25:17.520
Although—
well, I'm not going to get into that.

172
00:25:17.520 --> 00:25:22.120
But code that the executed—
as the executable text of today

173
00:25:22.120 --> 00:25:25.440
that does effect reality further

174
00:25:25.480 --> 00:25:28.800
than a change in attitude towards the task

175
00:25:29.800 --> 00:25:30.640
they effect.

176
00:25:30.640 --> 00:25:33.680
Physical reality also produces its own

177
00:25:33.720 --> 00:25:38.560
excess, its own code
with poetic intention

178
00:25:38.560 --> 00:25:42.440
that follows formal patterns
beyond functional ones.

179
00:25:42.840 --> 00:25:45.480
I hope I stuck to time.

180
00:25:45.480 --> 00:25:48.400
This is kind of the summary of my point.

181
00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:51.400
I hope that makes sense.
